  "limitations": [
    "Only V0-to-V1 was timed per scale, on 10-14 scored users and 78-261 requests; other edges have no measured runtime yet.",
    "Canary compute includes verify=True (three additional Full-cache reconstructions and control reads per request); formal execution omits these, so this overhead is conservatively retained in the estimate.",
    "Formal cohorts contain many more users and should amortize launches better; asynchronous query density and long-tail users can also change the critical path. No bulk-population GPU rate is claimed.",
    "Append-density adjustment treats all measured work as if it scales with the larger request/history/append proxy; no append-vs-repair phase timing is available to fit separate coefficients.",
    "History I/O is extrapolated separately from retained prior population runs. Cold filesystem cache, concurrent load, JIT warmup, OOM batch reduction, restarts, aggregation and plotting can move actual time outside this range.",
    "GPU memory figures are observed configured maximum-shape stress and micro-canary peaks; two equal-shape stress cycles showed no retained live allocation growth, not a long-uptime guarantee. CPU RSS was not measured.",
    "Canary quality controls are numerical checks; these tiny development cohorts are not motivation quality results. Saved Full and full repair of canonical serving order can differ at timestamp ties, and the discrepancy is retained.",
    "A prospective deviation-score roundoff-tie correction may change source hashes after these timings; it adds only pointwise selection work, but these timings remain measurements of the recorded probe source versions."
